1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,100
Does the runoff system hold back extremists? Well, this is exactly the fascinating question

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:09,460
that drives today's educational overview video.

3
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:14,580
We are about to dive into a truly incredible political economy paper, which shows us

4
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:20,260
how a seemingly technical, almost boring electoral rule can actually completely overturn

5
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,379
local politics and our everyday lives.

6
00:00:23,379 --> 00:00:28,740
With a tone we want to keep energetic but absolutely accurate, we are going to compare

7
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:33,859
the single-round system with the runoff system, we will see how that famous 15,000-inhabitant threshold works

8
00:00:33,859 --> 00:00:38,740
in Italian municipalities, and we will discover why, paradoxically, having more candidates

9
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:43,980
in the race means having less power at the extremes, and, believe it or not, even lower fiscal

10
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:44,980
volatility.

11
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:48,219
In short, details matter, let's get straight to the heart of it.

12
00:00:48,219 --> 00:00:52,219
So, let's start with the basics and compare the two systems.

13
00:00:52,219 --> 00:00:57,420
On one hand, we have the single-round system, a winner-take-all race: whoever gets even just a single vote

14
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:01,060
more than their opponents wins, no ifs, ands, or buts.

15
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:03,100
On the other hand, there is the runoff.

16
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:07,780
If in the first round no one obtains an absolute majority, the two most voted candidates go to

17
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,939
a second round, a true head-to-head clash.

18
00:01:10,939 --> 00:01:13,500
It might seem like just a bureaucratic technicality, right?

19
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Yet, as we will see, this small detail hides a mechanism capable of radically altering

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,700
the way politicians behave and form their alliances.

21
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:28,299
All of this brings us to a natural experiment literally served on a silver platter,

22
00:01:28,299 --> 00:01:31,379
which took shape in Italy starting in the early nineties.

23
00:01:31,379 --> 00:01:34,379
The law drew an invisible but sharp line.

24
00:01:34,379 --> 00:01:39,540
Municipalities with under 15,000 inhabitants vote using the single-round system, while those over 15,000

25
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:41,740
inhabitants use the runoff system.

26
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:45,379
For researchers, this division has been a real goldmine.

27
00:01:45,379 --> 00:01:50,219
They were able to compare towns that are practically identical in terms of economy, culture, and geography,

28
00:01:50,219 --> 00:01:53,659
yet separated by a single, crucial rule of the game.

29
00:01:53,659 --> 00:01:56,340
And here a fantastic paradox emerges.

30
00:01:56,340 --> 00:02:00,939
Logic would suggest that a two-round system, since there is more room to try one's luck,

31
00:02:00,939 --> 00:02:06,340
would favor chaos, dispersion, and perhaps give louder megaphones to radical voices.

32
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:11,379
So the question arises naturally: is it really possible to have more mayoral candidates

33
00:02:11,379 --> 00:02:14,259
and at the same time less political extremism?

34
00:02:14,259 --> 00:02:16,819
The data's answer is a resounding yes.

35
00:02:16,859 --> 00:02:20,259
The runoff literally crowds the political scene with candidates,

36
00:02:20,259 --> 00:02:24,620
but incredibly translates into much more moderate management of the city.

37
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:30,780
And the numbers confirm this anomaly in a way that leaves no room for doubt: 29%.

38
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:34,900
Moving to municipalities just a whisper above the 15,000-inhabitant threshold,

39
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:39,900
there is a 29% leap in the number of mayoral candidates,

40
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:45,299
compared to those just below the threshold. It is a huge increase; it means that the two-round system

41
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,820
really encourages more people to get involved.

42
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:50,700
And yet, despite this avalanche of candidates,

43
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:54,660
the influence of the most extreme factions plummets.

44
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:55,740
But why?

45
00:02:55,740 --> 00:03:00,380
Okay, let's look at the game theory behind it: it is fascinating.

46
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:05,380
In the first round of the runoff, moderate parties know they have the numbers on their side;

47
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:10,580
they can easily afford to run alone without signing pacts with the devil.

48
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:13,020
They know very well that if they make it to the second round,

49
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,900
the voters of the excluded extremist parties

50
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,900
will vote for the moderate candidate closest to them,

51
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:21,300
just to prevent the opposing side from winning.

52
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:25,140
As a result, the extremes are cut out of the negotiations that matter,

53
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:28,420
and moderates win without being subjected to political blackmail.

54
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:31,939
In the single-round system, however, the tune changes brutally.

55
00:03:31,939 --> 00:03:36,420
Since victory is achieved in one go and every single vote weighs heavily,

56
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:40,939
a moderate candidate absolutely cannot afford to split the vote.

57
00:03:40,979 --> 00:03:45,139
They are mathematically forced to form alliances and make compromises

58
00:03:45,139 --> 00:03:47,939
with the most extreme factions before the vote even takes place.

59
00:03:47,939 --> 00:03:51,020
If they don't, the radical faction runs on its own,

60
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:55,340
steals those few crucial votes, and hands victory to the opponents.

61
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:57,740
That is why here, in this system,

62
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:01,020
the extreme wings have crazy bargaining power.

63
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:03,819
In support of all this, the researcher has isolated

64
00:04:03,819 --> 00:04:06,020
a very clear empirical observation.

65
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:08,699
Looking at the historical data of the Italian Communist Party,

66
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:12,140
analyzed in a completely neutral way to understand the dynamics

67
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:14,220
of the most politically polarized factions,

68
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:16,060
they noticed a curious thing.

69
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:18,420
Under the runoff system, these types of factions

70
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:22,180
are twice as likely to run in elections on their own.

71
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,660
Sure, this way they measure their true electoral strength,

72
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:28,460
but they completely lose the ability to dictate the agenda to the future mayor,

73
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:31,259
because their votes are not essential to win the first round.

74
00:04:31,259 --> 00:04:32,740
So, summing up,

75
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:35,460
the runoff produces two crystal-clear effects:

76
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:37,259
an increase in mayoral candidates,

77
00:04:37,259 --> 00:04:39,860
and a drastic drop in power for the extremes;

78
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,860
it practically acts as a highly powerful filter.

79
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:44,860
It strips radical factions of their megaphone

80
00:04:44,860 --> 00:04:47,060
during alliance building.

81
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:48,740
And the most amazing thing of all

82
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:50,939
is that this happens even if voters' preferences

83
00:04:50,939 --> 00:04:52,779
remain identical.

84
00:04:52,779 --> 00:04:54,379
It is not that people change their minds,

85
00:04:54,379 --> 00:04:56,180
it is simply the rule of the game

86
00:04:56,180 --> 00:04:57,779
that defuses extremism

87
00:04:57,779 --> 00:05:00,819
and inevitably pushes politics toward the center.

88
00:05:00,819 --> 00:05:03,500
Section 1: Fiscal Volatility

89
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:05,259
and the Impact on Citizens.

90
00:05:05,259 --> 00:05:07,300
Now, you might be wondering, okay,

91
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,980
but what difference does this make to me in my everyday life?

92
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:11,300
And how!

93
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:14,259
Because the true consequence of this political theory

94
00:05:14,259 --> 00:05:16,699
is felt directly in everyone's wallet:

95
00:05:16,699 --> 00:05:17,699
taxes.

96
00:05:17,699 --> 00:05:20,180
Specifically, the research analyzes the municipal property tax

97
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:22,019
on business properties

98
00:05:22,019 --> 00:05:23,659
introduced in the 90s,

99
00:05:23,659 --> 00:05:26,420
a hot topic in any city hall.

100
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:29,099
To analyze tax fluctuations,

101
00:05:29,099 --> 00:05:31,339
researchers observed raw, hard empirical data

102
00:05:31,339 --> 00:05:33,539
from municipal budgets;

103
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:35,460
without taking anybody's side,

104
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:37,660
the statistical trend is as follows.

105
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:40,420
Center-left administrations tend to levy

106
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:42,939
higher tax rates on business properties

107
00:05:42,939 --> 00:05:44,860
to fund public services,

108
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:46,900
while center-right administrations

109
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:48,180
tend to lower them.

110
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,420
I repeat, this is not a value judgment;

111
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:53,860
it is a mathematical fact observed in the budgets of Italian municipalities.

112
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:55,660
Well, imagine what happens

113
00:05:55,660 --> 00:05:57,820
when the political color in town changes.

114
00:05:57,820 --> 00:05:59,100
In single-round municipalities,

115
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:00,540
a passing of the torch causes

116
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:02,300
a fiscal rollercoaster.

117
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:03,939
Taxes oscillate violently

118
00:06:03,939 --> 00:06:06,340
because the mayor, held hostage by extreme factions,

119
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:09,379
must give in to their demands for drastic changes.

120
00:06:09,379 --> 00:06:11,420
Conversely, in municipalities with runoffs,

121
00:06:11,420 --> 00:06:12,579
where the mayor is moderate

122
00:06:12,579 --> 00:06:14,620
and has no coalition debts to extremists,

123
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:17,460
taxes remain incredibly flat and stable,

124
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:19,220
even if the city switches from right

125
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:20,900
to left or vice versa.

126
00:06:20,900 --> 00:06:23,019
The quantitative impact of this stability

127
00:06:23,019 --> 00:06:24,819
is nothing short of sensational:

128
00:06:24,819 --> 00:06:26,460
61%.

129
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:29,460
The study tells us that, in municipalities with runoffs,

130
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:30,860
tax volatility

131
00:06:30,860 --> 00:06:33,900
over time plummets by 61%.

132
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:36,139
Imagine being a business owner.

133
00:06:36,139 --> 00:06:38,139
You go from having absolutely no idea

134
00:06:38,139 --> 00:06:40,139
how much tax you will pay next year

135
00:06:40,139 --> 00:06:42,500
to a predictable and safe environment.

136
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:44,980
This stability is the direct result of governments

137
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:46,620
that are not held hostage,

138
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:50,379
free from the constant pressure of radical allies.

139
00:06:50,379 --> 00:06:52,620
And watch out, it doesn't end there.

140
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:55,819
Researchers also measured cross-sectional volatility,

141
00:06:55,819 --> 00:06:57,740
meaning the difference in tax levels

142
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:00,660
between very similar municipalities in the exact same year.

143
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:03,060
Here too, the drop is steep:

144
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:05,060
71% less.

145
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:06,939
This means that the runoff does not just

146
00:07:06,939 --> 00:07:08,860
calm the waters in a single municipality,

147
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:12,060
but harmonizes and makes economic policies consistent

148
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:13,260
across different towns,

149
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:16,260
creating an atmosphere of widespread stability.

150
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:17,460
At this point,

151
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:19,900
it is only natural to play devil's advocate.

152
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:21,620
Could it be that taxes are more stable

153
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:23,140
simply because, under the runoff system,

154
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:25,460
mayors stay in office longer?

155
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:26,660
An excellent objection

156
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:28,460
that the study immediately tested.

157
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:29,860
But the data says no.

158
00:07:29,860 --> 00:07:32,660
Political turnover is virtually identical.

159
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:35,259
Mayors in municipalities with over fifteen thousand inhabitants

160
00:07:35,259 --> 00:07:36,860
do not stay in office any longer.

161
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:38,660
Governments fall and are renewed

162
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:40,220
with the exact same frequency

163
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:41,460
in both systems.

164
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:43,420
Fiscal stability is not determined by who

165
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:44,660
is glued to the seat,

166
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:47,259
but precisely by the moderate policy approach.

167
00:07:47,259 --> 00:07:48,860
If we were to tattoo

168
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:52,060
the conclusion of this study on ourselves, it would be this quote:

169
00:07:52,060 --> 00:07:54,060
"The runoff system reduces the influence

170
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:56,259
of extremists and induces moderation."

171
00:07:56,259 --> 00:07:58,660
The causal link is proven.

172
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:00,460
If you strip extreme factions of

173
00:08:00,460 --> 00:08:03,460
the power to blackmail you when building alliances,

174
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:05,220
you immediately get a local economy

175
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:06,860
that is more solid and predictable.

176
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:08,860
This is not far-fetched theory;

177
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:11,260
it is a highly powerful institutional mechanism

178
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:12,060
that shields

179
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:14,860
our economy from unnecessary shocks.

180
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:15,860
Section 2.

181
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:17,460
A curb on extremism:

182
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:19,460
Lessons for Institutions.

183
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:20,860
Let's take a step back

184
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:23,660
and look at the forest instead of the single tree.

185
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:25,860
This massive Italian natural experiment

186
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:26,860
is a golden lesson

187
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:29,460
for the design of democracies worldwide.

188
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:30,660
Understanding how simply tweaking

189
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:33,060
a voting rule can defuse polarization

190
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:34,460
is a crucial discovery,

191
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:35,860
especially nowadays,

192
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:38,860
where political extremism seems to be making headway almost everywhere,

193
00:08:38,860 --> 00:08:41,860
in developed as well as emerging nations.

194
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:43,460
After all, we all know very well

195
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:45,659
that unchecked political extremism

196
00:08:45,659 --> 00:08:46,860
causes huge damage.

197
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:49,259
The research reminds us of this in no uncertain terms:

198
00:08:49,259 --> 00:08:51,060
it reduces overall well-being,

199
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:52,659
especially for those seeking stability

200
00:08:52,659 --> 00:08:54,060
to live and work.

201
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:55,659
It generates a toxic social climate

202
00:08:55,659 --> 00:08:57,259
full of constant disagreement,

203
00:08:57,259 --> 00:09:00,259
and, worse yet, it paralyzes decision-making processes.

204
00:09:00,259 --> 00:09:02,459
A government blocked by the vetoes of extreme factions

205
00:09:02,459 --> 00:09:03,659
simply does not make decisions,

206
00:09:03,659 --> 00:09:05,459
harming the entire community.

207
00:09:05,459 --> 00:09:07,659
And this leaves us with a final question

208
00:09:07,659 --> 00:09:09,259
that is truly food for thought.

209
00:09:09,259 --> 00:09:11,860
If a bureaucratic threshold of 15,000 inhabitants,

210
00:09:11,860 --> 00:09:13,459
a number on a piece of paper,

211
00:09:13,459 --> 00:09:15,659
has the power to so profoundly disrupt

212
00:09:15,659 --> 00:09:17,860
the economy and politics of an entire city,

213
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:19,360
what other invisible rules

214
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:21,659
are quietly shaping our society?

215
00:09:21,659 --> 00:09:23,659
How much of what we experience every day

216
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:26,059
is dictated by the hidden institutional gears

217
00:09:26,059 --> 00:09:28,059
that we take completely for granted?

218
00:09:28,059 --> 00:09:29,659
It is a provocative thought

219
00:09:29,659 --> 00:09:31,159
that invites us to look at the very foundations

220
00:09:31,159 --> 00:09:32,860
of our democracies

221
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:34,659
with completely new eyes.